# micro_gotchas.log — player-POV "how the game actually handles this" details
# (STATE_CONSOLIDATION.md §4), distinct from tools/wiki-kb/gotchas.log's research-process
# findings. One line each, own-words, wiki- or mechanic-grounded (cited) or "??". Append-only.

[tick] Game tick = 0.6s server cycle (Game_tick lead); running covers 2 tiles/tick vs 1/tick walking, so a loop's travel legs cost roughly half the ticks under Run — a checklist should say "run" explicitly on any leg longer than a few tiles, not leave it implicit.
[tick-3t] 3-tick spell loops (Superheat Item, Plank Make, Bake Pie) chain-cast on a full inventory run ~1800 casts/hour in practice vs a theoretical 1980 at zero downtime (Game_tick "Application" section) — cite the ~7-10% real-world haircut for these specific spells' xp/hr, not the naive tick-math number.
[tick-1t] 1-tick prayer flicking means toggling the SAME prayer off-then-back-on within every single 0.6s tick window, and only pays off for multi-combat zones or enemies with varying attack speed (e.g. cave horrors) that can't be lazy-flicked; the quick-prayer orb is normally used for it, not manual prayer-book clicks (Prayer.s15/s16).
[prayer-flick] Prayer drain is calculated once per tick, but the game exempts the ACTIVATION tick itself from drain — lazy/4-tick flicking (toggle on the tick you're hit or you attack, toggle off the next tick) is the realistic default for a guide instruction, not "hold the prayer on for the whole fight" (Prayer.s14/s16 tick-by-tick table).
[aggro] Aggressive monsters go tolerant (stop attacking unprovoked) after 10 continuous minutes in their vicinity, but logging out — INCLUDING a world-hop — does NOT reset that timer, only physically leaving the area does (Aggressiveness "Notes"); "hop to reset aggro" is a wrong instruction, hop only for crowding.
[aggro] Aggro is combat-level gated: a level-based-aggressive monster attacks only while your combat level ≤ 2x its own level; above that it ignores you outright (Aggressiveness lead) — a training-spot step should flag the combat level at which the spot stops being AFK-viable, since the player will simply outlevel the aggro.
[run-energy] Run energy is an internal 0-10,000-unit pool displayed as a rounded-down percentage, and walking never drains it even with Run toggled on (Run_energy "Using energy") — "conserve energy" should mean "walk the short legs, run the long ones," not toggling Run off for an entire loop.
[noted-items] A bank note is a separate stackable item id from its unnoted counterpart and always takes exactly 1 inventory slot regardless of quantity; storing/selling/using it on a bank booth or banker unnotes it with no PIN required (Noted_item "Conversion") — withdraw noted by default for any gather-loop product that isn't consumed unnoted in the same trip.
[noted-items] Not every tradeable item is noteable (the bank interface hides the option for a short named-exception list, e.g. mysterious emblems) — a step that assumes "withdraw noted" for an arbitrary target should verify the item actually has a noted variant, else default to unnoted and mark it "??" if unchecked.
[inventory-full] This project's own atoms already carry the policy per-row as a keep-drop hint (GRANULARITY §4: "keep:a,b;drop:rest") — an inventory-full moment with no authored hint should default to banking via the nearest booth over dropping, since drop-vs-bank-vs-burn is advisory, never a completion-relevant verb.
[herb-state] Herbs gather/buy grimy and need exactly one clean click (no bank trip, no bank access needed) before an Herblore recipe or an identification-level check will accept them — cleaning while walking to the bank is a real zero-time embed, already modeled on pps-05-withdraw-brew's do-while hint.
[herb-state] An unfinished potion (vial + herb, secondary not yet added) is a DISTINCT held item from its finished N-dose counterpart — a brew loop that mixes a full inventory of unf potions in one pass and adds secondaries in a second pass needs its own inventory accounting for both states; state_scan.mjs tracks "_unf" and finished slugs as separate keys for exactly this reason.
[click-order] Standard use-X-on-Y click order is: click the ITEM in the inventory first (it visibly "sticks" to the cursor), THEN click the target item/NPC/object — clicking the target first just examines or walks to it, it does not queue the interaction.
[teleport-charge] Charge-limited teleport items (rings, some amulets, tablets) drain per USE, not per destination choice within a multi-destination item — check the remaining charge count before a supply run rather than assuming "one trip = one charge," since a mid-loop stranding costs more time than the check.
[teleport-charge] Teleport tablets are single-use and consumed on click with no partial refund, while a teleport SPELL consumes its runes on a completed cast regardless of where you're going — a loadout's teleport budget should be counted in one-way trips, never round trips.
[world-hop] World-hopping is a reconnect, not a respawn: exact tile position, inventory, and equipped gear all persist across the hop — safe to use purely for resource/monster-spot crowding (GRANULARITY's contested-fallback hint) without side effects on aggro-tolerance timers (still counts as "not leaving the area," gotcha above) or a farming patch's growth stage.
[stamina] A stamina potion dose restores 20% run energy AND cuts run-energy depletion 70% for 2 minutes (non-stacking — redosing early just resets the 2-minute timer, doesn't add to it); the run-energy orb turns orange while active, a real visual cue worth citing in any long-run loop's hints (Stamina_potion lead).
[herb-state] Farming 77 turns a super energy potion + amylase crystal into a stamina potion (25.5 Herblore xp/dose) — on an ironman route this Herblore-77 gate means "buy stamina" isn't an option and the gather/produce chain for amylase crystals (Managing Miscellania) has to be sourced ahead of any run-heavy loop that assumes stamina potions are available (Stamina_potion "Ironmen access").
